  "result": "I found and fixed the cause, and all 8 tests pass (`python3 -m unittest`).\n\n**Cause:** In `shop/storage.py:17`, `save_order` is meant to load the existing rows, drop the one with the same id as the order being saved, then add the new version. The filter had the comparison backwards (`r[\"id\"] == order.id`), so it kept only the matching row and threw away all the others. A new order has no matching row, so every save replaced the whole file with just that order.\n\n**Fix:** I changed the comparison to `!=`. Saving now keeps every other order and replaces only an earlier copy of the same order.\n\n**Tests:** I added `tests/test_storage.py` with two cases:\n- saving two orders and checking that `load()` returns both;\n- saving the same order again after a state change and checking there's still one row, with the updated state.\n\nNothing has been committed yet.",
